#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-repack(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:7
msgid "git-repack - Pack unpacked objects in a repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:13
msgid ""
"'git repack' [-a] [-A] [-d] [-f] [-F] [-l] [-n] [-q] [-b] [-m] "
"[--window=<n>] [--depth=<n>] [--threads=<n>] [--keep-pack=<pack-name>] "
"[--write-midx]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:20
msgid ""
"This command is used to combine all objects that do not currently reside in "
"a \"pack\", into a pack.  It can also be used to re-organize existing packs "
"into a single, more efficient pack."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:24
msgid ""
"A pack is a collection of objects, individually compressed, with delta "
"compression applied, stored in a single file, with an associated index file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:27
msgid ""
"Packs are used to reduce the load on mirror systems, backup engines, disk "
"storage, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-a::`\n"
"\tInstead of incrementally packing the unpacked objects,\n"
"\tpack everything referenced into a single pack.\n"
"\tEspecially useful when packing a repository that is used\n"
"\tfor private development. Use\n"
"\twith `-d`.  This will clean up the objects that `git prune`\n"
"\tleaves behind, but `git fsck --full --dangling` shows as\n"
"\tdangling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:43
msgid ""
"Note that users fetching over dumb protocols will have to fetch the whole "
"new pack in order to get any contained object, no matter how many other "
"objects in that pack they already have locally."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:48
msgid ""
"Promisor packfiles are repacked separately: if there are packfiles that have "
"an associated \".promisor\" file, these packfiles will be repacked into "
"another separate pack, and an empty \".promisor\" file corresponding to the "
"new separate pack will be written."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:59
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-A::`\n"
"\tSame as `-a`, unless `-d` is used.  Then any unreachable\n"
"\tobjects in a previous pack become loose, unpacked objects,\n"
"\tinstead of being left in the old pack.  Unreachable objects\n"
"\tare never intentionally added to a pack, even when repacking.\n"
"\tThis option prevents unreachable objects from being immediately\n"
"\tdeleted by way of being left in the old pack and then\n"
"\tremoved.  Instead, the loose unreachable objects\n"
"\twill be pruned according to normal expiry rules\n"
"\twith the next 'git gc' invocation. See linkgit:git-gc[1].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:65
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-d::`\n"
"\tAfter packing, if the newly created packs make some\n"
"\texisting packs redundant, remove the redundant packs.\n"
"\tAlso run  'git prune-packed' to remove redundant\n"
"\tloose object files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:71
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--cruft::`\n"
"\tSame as `-a`, unless `-d` is used. Then any unreachable objects\n"
"\tare packed into a separate cruft pack. Unreachable objects can\n"
"\tbe pruned using the normal expiry rules with the next `git gc`\n"
"\tinvocation (see linkgit:git-gc[1]). Incompatible with `-k`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:76
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--cruft-expiration=<approxidate>::`\n"
"\tExpire unreachable objects older than `<approxidate>`\n"
"\timmediately instead of waiting for the next `git gc` invocation.\n"
"\tOnly useful with `--cruft -d`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:87
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--max-cruft-size=<n>::`\n"
"\tRepack cruft objects into packs as large as `<n>` bytes before\n"
"\tcreating new packs. As long as there are enough cruft packs\n"
"\tsmaller than `<n>`, repacking will cause a new cruft pack to\n"
"\tbe created containing objects from any combined cruft packs,\n"
"\talong with any new unreachable objects. Cruft packs larger than\n"
"\t`<n>` will not be modified. When the new cruft pack is larger\n"
"\tthan `<n>` bytes, it will be split into multiple packs, all of\n"
"\twhich are guaranteed to be at most `<n>` bytes in size. Only\n"
"\tuseful with `--cruft -d`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:93
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--expire-to=<dir>::`\n"
"\tWrite a cruft pack containing pruned objects (if any) to the\n"
"\tdirectory `<dir>`. This option is useful for keeping a copy of\n"
"\tany pruned objects in a separate directory as a backup. Only\n"
"\tuseful with `--cruft -d`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:97
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-l::`\n"
"\tPass the `--local` option to 'git pack-objects'. See\n"
"\tlinkgit:git-pack-objects[1].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:101
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-f::`\n"
"\tPass the `--no-reuse-delta` option to `git-pack-objects`, see\n"
"\tlinkgit:git-pack-objects[1].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:105
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-F::`\n"
"\tPass the `--no-reuse-object` option to `git-pack-objects`, see\n"
"\tlinkgit:git-pack-objects[1].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:110
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-q::`\n"
"`--quiet::`\n"
"\tShow no progress over the standard error stream and pass the `-q`\n"
"\toption to 'git pack-objects'. See linkgit:git-pack-objects[1].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:117
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-n::`\n"
"\tDo not update the server information with\n"
"\t'git update-server-info'.  This option skips\n"
"\tupdating local catalog files needed to publish\n"
"\tthis repository (or a direct copy of it)\n"
"\tover HTTP or FTP.  See linkgit:git-update-server-info[1].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--window=<n>::`\n"
"`--depth=<n>::`\n"
"\tThese two options affect how the objects contained in the pack are\n"
"\tstored using delta compression. The objects are first internally\n"
"\tsorted by type, size and optionally names and compared against the\n"
"\tother objects within `--window` to see if using delta compression saves\n"
"\tspace. `--depth` limits the maximum delta depth; making it too deep\n"
"\taffects the performance on the unpacker side, because delta data needs\n"
"\tto be applied that many times to get to the necessary object.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:130
msgid ""
"The default value for --window is 10 and --depth is 50. The maximum depth is "
"4095."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--threads=<n>::`\n"
"\tThis option is passed through to `git pack-objects`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:146
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--window-memory=<n>::`\n"
"\tThis option provides an additional limit on top of `--window`;\n"
"\tthe window size will dynamically scale down so as to not take\n"
"\tup more than '<n>' bytes in memory.  This is useful in\n"
"\trepositories with a mix of large and small objects to not run\n"
"\tout of memory with a large window, but still be able to take\n"
"\tadvantage of the large window for the smaller objects.  The\n"
"\tsize can be suffixed with \"k\", \"m\", or \"g\".\n"
"\t`--window-memory=0` makes memory usage unlimited.  The default\n"
"\tis taken from the `pack.windowMemory` configuration variable.\n"
"\tNote that the actual memory usage will be the limit multiplied\n"
"\tby the number of threads used by linkgit:git-pack-objects[1].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:156
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--max-pack-size=<n>::`\n"
"\tMaximum size of each output pack file. The size can be suffixed with\n"
"\t\"k\", \"m\", or \"g\". The minimum size allowed is limited to 1 MiB.\n"
"\tIf specified, multiple packfiles may be created, which also\n"
"\tprevents the creation of a bitmap index.\n"
"\tThe default is unlimited, unless the config variable\n"
"\t`pack.packSizeLimit` is set. Note that this option may result in\n"
"\ta larger and slower repository; see the discussion in\n"
"\t`pack.packSizeLimit`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:168
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--filter=<filter-spec>::`\n"
"\tRemove objects matching the filter specification from the\n"
"\tresulting packfile and put them into a separate packfile. Note\n"
"\tthat objects used in the working directory are not filtered\n"
"\tout. So for the split to fully work, it's best to perform it\n"
"\tin a bare repo and to use the `-a` and `-d` options along with\n"
"\tthis option.  Also `--no-write-bitmap-index` (or the\n"
"\t`repack.writebitmaps` config option set to `false`) should be\n"
"\tused otherwise writing bitmap index will fail, as it supposes\n"
"\ta single packfile containing all the objects. See\n"
"\tlinkgit:git-rev-list[1] for valid `<filter-spec>` forms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:179
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--filter-to=<dir>::`\n"
"\tWrite the pack containing filtered out objects to the\n"
"\tdirectory `<dir>`. Only useful with `--filter`. This can be\n"
"\tused for putting the pack on a separate object directory that\n"
"\tis accessed through the Git alternates mechanism. **WARNING:**\n"
"\tIf the packfile containing the filtered out objects is not\n"
"\taccessible, the repo can become corrupt as it might not be\n"
"\tpossible to access the objects in that packfile. See the\n"
"\t`objects` and `objects/info/alternates` sections of\n"
"\tlinkgit:gitrepository-layout[5].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:188
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-b::`\n"
"`--write-bitmap-index::`\n"
"\tWrite a reachability bitmap index as part of the repack. This\n"
"\tonly makes sense when used with `-a`, `-A` or `-m`, as the bitmaps\n"
"\tmust be able to refer to all reachable objects. This option\n"
"\toverrides the setting of `repack.writeBitmaps`. This option\n"
"\thas no effect if multiple packfiles are created, unless writing a\n"
"\tMIDX (in which case a multi-pack bitmap is created).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:197
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--pack-kept-objects::`\n"
"\tInclude objects in `.keep` files when repacking.  Note that we\n"
"\tstill do not delete `.keep` packs after `pack-objects` finishes.\n"
"\tThis means that we may duplicate objects, but this makes the\n"
"\toption safe to use when there are concurrent pushes or fetches.\n"
"\tThis option is generally only useful if you are writing bitmaps\n"
"\twith `-b` or `repack.writeBitmaps`, as it ensures that the\n"
"\tbitmapped packfile has the necessary objects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:204
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--keep-pack=<pack-name>::`\n"
"\tExclude the given pack from repacking. This is the equivalent\n"
"\tof having `.keep` file on the pack. `<pack-name>` is the\n"
"\tpack file name without leading directory (e.g. `pack-123.pack`).\n"
"\tThe option can be specified multiple times to keep multiple\n"
"\tpacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:210
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--unpack-unreachable=<when>::`\n"
"\tWhen loosening unreachable objects, do not bother loosening any\n"
"\tobjects older than `<when>`. This can be used to optimize out\n"
"\tthe write of any objects that would be immediately pruned by\n"
"\ta follow-up `git prune`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:217
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-k::`\n"
"`--keep-unreachable::`\n"
"\tWhen used with `-ad`, any unreachable objects from existing\n"
"\tpacks will be appended to the end of the packfile instead of\n"
"\tbeing removed. In addition, any unreachable loose objects will\n"
"\tbe packed (and their loose counterparts removed).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:222
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-i::`\n"
"`--delta-islands::`\n"
"\tPass the `--delta-islands` option to `git-pack-objects`, see\n"
"\tlinkgit:git-pack-objects[1].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:228
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-g<factor>::`\n"
"`--geometric=<factor>::`\n"
"\tArrange resulting pack structure so that each successive pack\n"
"\tcontains at least `<factor>` times the number of objects as the\n"
"\tnext-largest pack.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:234
msgid ""
"`git repack` ensures this by determining a \"cut\" of packfiles that need to "
"be repacked into one in order to ensure a geometric progression. It picks "
"the smallest set of packfiles such that as many of the larger packfiles (by "
"count of objects contained in that pack) may be left intact."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:239
msgid ""
"Unlike other repack modes, the set of objects to pack is determined uniquely "
"by the set of packs being \"rolled-up\"; in other words, the packs "
"determined to need to be combined in order to restore a geometric "
"progression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:242
msgid ""
"Loose objects are implicitly included in this \"roll-up\", without respect "
"to their reachability. This is subject to change in the future."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:246
msgid ""
"When writing a multi-pack bitmap, `git repack` selects the largest resulting "
"pack as the preferred pack for object selection by the MIDX (see "
"linkgit:git-multi-pack-index[1])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:251
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-m::`\n"
"`--write-midx::`\n"
"\tWrite a multi-pack index (see linkgit:git-multi-pack-index[1])\n"
"\tcontaining the non-redundant packs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:253
#, no-wrap
msgid "CONFIGURATION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:257
msgid ""
"Various configuration variables affect packing, see linkgit:git-config[1] "
"(search for \"pack\" and \"delta\")."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:267
msgid ""
"By default, the command passes `--delta-base-offset` option to 'git "
"pack-objects'; this typically results in slightly smaller packs, but the "
"generated packs are incompatible with versions of Git older than version "
"1.4.4. If you need to share your repository with such ancient Git versions, "
"either directly or via the dumb http protocol, then you need to set the "
"configuration variable `repack.UseDeltaBaseOffset` to \"false\" and "
"repack. Access from old Git versions over the native protocol is unaffected "
"by this option as the conversion is performed on the fly as needed in that "
"case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:271
msgid ""
"Delta compression is not used on objects larger than the "
"`core.bigFileThreshold` configuration variable and on files with the "
"attribute `delta` set to false."
msgstr ""

